{"name":"Auger Shell","hex":"#9f9292","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"auger","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A carpenter's tool for boring holes longer than those bored by a gimlet.","A snake or plumber's snake ."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To use an auger; to drill a hole using an auger.","To proceed in the manner of an auger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/auger"},{"term":"shell","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard external covering of an animal.\nThe calcareous or chitinous external covering of mollusks, crustaceans, and some other invertebrates.","The calcareous or chitinous external covering of mollusks, crustaceans, and some other invertebrates."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To remove the outer covering or shell of something.","To bombard, to fire projectiles at, especially with artillery."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shell"}],"entries":[{"title":"Terebridae","blocks":[{"text":"The Terebridae, commonly referred to as auger shells or auger snails, is a family of predatory marine gastropods in the superfamily Conoidea. They have extremely high-spired shells with numerous whorls; their common name refers to the resemblance of their shells to rock-drill bit. More than 400 species are recently known worldwide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Acus_maculatus.jpg/330px-Acus_maculatus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terebridae","wikidata":"Q967678"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Atlantic_auger_0008.jpg/250px-Atlantic_auger_0008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4e/Atlantic_auger_0008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikiped